From 27861993c54a7fb9f74ab61931b58d424b12e9b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:04:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 51/52] drm/gma500: lay sgxfive render targets out by size, up
 to 4096x4096

Render targets with more than eight 16-pixel tile rows faulted on page 0
of the parameter slot: the kernel used the tiling words and buffer sizes
the closed driver used for a 64x64 target at every size. Captures of the
closed driver at 241 sizes, from 1x1 to 4096x4096, show that it lays the
target out by size: 32x16-pixel tiles grouped into two macrotiles across
and down, or four from 640x480 pixels on, each a whole number of 4x4-tile
blocks. TE_MTILE1/2 hold the macrotile size and boundaries, TE_MTILE its
block count; the RT data set's macrotile width, the RT data's blocks per
macrotile, region array and tail-pointer sizes, and the region-header
buffers follow from the same layout. The parameter buffer does not grow.

Add sgx_render_layout(), which reproduces all 241 captures, require a
render command's TE words to be the layout of its MTE_SCREEN size, set
the RT data's size words from the job's target when it runs (and when a
reset replays it), and size the TA-data buffers for the largest layout:
the TA_DATA slots grow from 64 KiB to 2 MiB, about 1 MiB of it used.
The render-target limit JOB_INFO reports becomes 4096x4096, the largest
the closed driver accepts.

On the laptop the first triangle renders exactly from 32x16 to 4096x4096,
non-power-of-two sizes included, and one context renders targets of
changing sizes in a row.

Assisted-by: LLM
